{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe mutation sandbox's Linux write-deny (`bwrap`) has **no gating CI lane**. The\nenforcement tests are gated on `skipif(mutation_sandbox.probe() is None)`. Bubblewrap\nis installed on the Linux lanes (commit `b63cf799`), but the runners deny unprivileged\n`CLONE_NEWUSER`, so the functional probe correctly returns `None`, every enforcement\ntest SKIPS, and the leg goes green having proven nothing about Linux enforcement.\n\nThis is the silent-skip failure mode: the signal that would report a broken Linux\nsandbox is indistinguishable from the signal for a healthy one.\n\n## The environment precondition, measured\n\nTwo independent mechanisms deny the namespace, and BOTH must be cleared:\n\n1. **Ubuntu 23.10+ AppArmor** sets `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`,\n   denying unprivileged `CLONE_NEWUSER`.\n2. **Container runtimes' default seccomp** blocks the `clone`/`unshare` outright — so a\n   plain `container:` job does NOT work, and neither does relaxing seccomp alone.\n\nMeasured on `ubuntu:24.04` (bubblewrap 0.9.0), running a real write through `bwrap`:\n\n| Container mode | Result |\n|---|---|\n| default | UNUSABLE — `bwrap` never starts |\n| `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` | UNUSABLE — `bwrap` never starts |\n| `--privileged` | **ENFORCING** — child ran, write outside the allow-list denied |\n\nThe probe that produced this table carries a LIVENESS check, and that is load-bearing:\na first version without one reported \"enforced\" for every mode, because a `bwrap` that\nfails to start never writes the file whose absence was being read as denial — the same\nfalse pass fixed under `c100-c4f1`. Any probe used here must assert the child ran.\n\n## Approach\n\nA **privileged container lane**. `--privileged` is a plain OCI runtime capability, not a\nCI-provider feature: the identical container runs on a developer laptop, which is what\n`project.portability` requires. The host alternative — `sudo sysctl -w\nkernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0` on a runner with root — is recorded as a\nfallback for environments that cannot run privileged containers, but is NOT the primary\nmechanism, because it was not measured here.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] A gating lane runs the `bwrap` enforcement tests with `probe()` returning a\n      mechanism, so they EXECUTE rather than skip. Verified by the lane's own report\n      showing those tests passed, not skipped.\n- [x] The lane asserts its own precondition BEFORE running tests: a liveness-checked\n      probe confirms `bwrap` both starts and denies a write outside its allow-list. If\n      it cannot, the lane FAILS with that message rather than proceeding to a green\n      run of skipped tests.\n- [x] A skipped enforcement test is a FAILURE on this lane, not a pass. Implemented by\n      an env flag that turns the `@live` skip into a hard requirement, so the silent-skip\n      mode cannot return. Verified by a test asserting that with the flag set and no\n      mechanism available, the suite fails rather than skips.\n- [x] The requirement flag is proven load-bearing: with it set and `probe()` forced to\n      return None, the enforcement tests FAIL. Demonstrated by real substitution.\n- [x] The mechanism is portable: it depends on an OCI runtime capability available\n      outside any single CI provider, and the same container is runnable locally\n      (`project.portability`). No GitHub-Actions-only construct carries the enforcement.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Linux `bwrap` write-denial is proven by an automated, gating control.\n- Skipping is impossible on that lane; a missing mechanism fails it loudly.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`.\n\n## Notes\n\nDiscovered from `e668-b496`: its plan review BLOCKED on a criterion claiming a green\n`ubuntu-latest` leg proves Linux enforcement when the tests there skip. The same gap was\nraised as an advisory by the Gerrit `LLM-Review` bot on change 2256 (patch sets 1, 3, 4)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787892432359396001,"uuid":"750dcb74-2042-4027-a5e8-716afbe1bdfa"}